    "title": "Rewire steerAuthority counters at the scheduler decision site (currently dead: steps=0 over 516k runs)",
    "description": "steerAuthority.{steps,preferenceExpressed,preferenceHonored,honored,blockedByTimerGate} serialize as all-zero across both seeds and 516k runs, including steps, which should tick once per scheduling step regardless of policy. Locate the increment sites in spur-core/src/simulator/core/scheduler.rs and util_stats.rs and determine whether (a) the struct is populated on a per-thread UtilStats that is never merged into the aggregate written by simulator/explorer.rs, or (b) the increments sit behind a cfg/feature or a config gate that is off in scheduler_configs/loop/general_vr.json. Fix the wiring so steps counts every step-selection call and preferenceExpressed/honored count at the point the scheduler consults any preference source. Add a debug_assert or a regression test asserting steps>0 for any run with stepsUsedSum>0, so this class of dead counter cannot regress silently again. No policy change.",
    "category": "tooling",
    "buildsOn": [
      "util_stats",
      "scheduler step loop",
      "explorer serialization"
    ],
    "expectedGain": 6,
    "expectedCost": 1,
    "rationale": "steps=0 with stepsUsedSum=779M is proof the aggregate never reaches serialization; every steer/admission hypothesis is being judged against a counter that is structurally incapable of being nonzero, so their falsifiers are vacuous. The steps>0 invariant test converts a recurring silent blind spot into a hard failure.",
    "generalityArgument": "Counts scheduler step-selection and preference-consultation events keyed by CFG vertex and delivery state; references no handler, message type, or protocol spec.",
